= B2
{c}
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B2}

For the <quantum harmonic oscillator>, the <canonical commutation relation> $[x,p]=i\hbar$ gives $[a,a^\dagger]=1$ and $H_0=\hbar\omega_0(a^\dagger a+1/2)$. Inverting the definition of the <annihilation operator> gives
$$
x=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar}{2m\omega_0}}(a+a^\dagger).
$$
With normalized <energy eigenstates> and consistent phases, $a|0\rangle=0$ and $a^\dagger|0\rangle=|1\rangle$. Their <orthogonality> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{|\langle1|x|0\rangle|=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar}{2m\omega_0}}\equiv x_{01}.}
$$
More generally, $\langle n|x|0\rangle=x_{01}\delta_{n1}$, the oscillator <electric-dipole selection rule> out of the <ground state>.

To derive the transition <amplitude>, insert the expansion in stationary <energy eigenstates> into the <Schrödinger equation> with $H=H_0+V_1(t)$. The derivatives of the explicit phases cancel the $H_0$ terms. Projection onto $\langle n|$ leaves the exact coefficient equations
$$
i\hbar\dot c_n(t)=\sum_r e^{i(E_n-E_r)t/\hbar}\langle n|V_1(t)|r\rangle c_r(t).
$$
The initial conditions are $c_0(-\infty)=1$ and $c_{n\ne0}(-\infty)=0$. To first order in the perturbation, replace the <amplitudes> on the right by these initial values. Since $E_n-E_0=n\hbar\omega_0$, integration gives <time-dependent perturbation theory> in the form
$$
\boxed{c_n^{(1)}(t)=\frac1{i\hbar}\int_{-\infty}^t e^{in\omega_0t'}\langle n|V_1(t')|0\rangle\,dt'\qquad(n\ne0).}
$$
This neglects depletion and higher-order transitions; it is justified when the total transition <probability> is small. Here $\langle0|x|0\rangle=0$, so there is also no first-order diagonal <amplitude> change from this particular perturbation.

For the exponential <electric field>, the <electric-dipole interaction> is $V_1=-qEx e^{t/\tau}$, with $\tau>0$. Only $n=1$ couples directly to the initial state, and
$$
c_1^{(1)}(0)=\frac{iqEx_{01}}\hbar\int_{-\infty}^0e^{(1/\tau+i\omega_0)t'}\,dt'.
$$
The <Born rule> gives
$$
\boxed{p_1(0)\simeq\frac{q^2E^2}{2\hbar m\omega_0}\left|\int_{-\infty}^0e^{i\omega_0t'}e^{t'/\tau}\,dt'\right|^2.}
$$
The real part $1/\tau>0$ makes the lower-limit contribution vanish. Thus
$$
\int_{-\infty}^0e^{(1/\tau+i\omega_0)t'}\,dt'=\frac1{1/\tau+i\omega_0},\qquad \boxed{p_1(0)\simeq\frac{q^2E^2\tau^2}{2\hbar m\omega_0[1+\omega_0^2\tau^2]}.}
$$
Set $u=\omega_0\tau$ and $\eta=qEx_{01}/(\hbar\omega_0)$. The dimensionless form is $p_1/\eta^2=u^2/(1+u^2)$, increasing monotonically from zero to one, with half the asymptotic value at $u=1$. A sufficient weak-field condition for the entire curve is $|\eta|\ll1$.

\Image[2018-oscillator-switch-on.png]
{title=Original-basis excitation approaches a finite limit during slow oscillator displacement}

For $\omega_0\tau\ll1$, the force has acted for too little time to appreciably change the state at the instant of measurement. The short-time <amplitude> is approximately $iqEx_{01}\tau/\hbar$, so
$$
\boxed{p_1(0)\simeq\frac{q^2E^2\tau^2}{2\hbar m\omega_0}\quad(\omega_0\tau\ll1).}
$$
For $\omega_0\tau\gg1$, instead,
$$
\boxed{p_1(0)\longrightarrow\frac{q^2E^2}{2\hbar m\omega_0^3}=\eta^2.}
$$
The nonzero slow-switch limit is a <probability> in the original $H_0$ basis. It does not contradict the <adiabatic theorem>. At $t=0$ the instantaneous oscillator is displaced by $d=qE/(m\omega_0^2)$:
$$
H(0)=\frac{p^2}{2m}+\frac12m\omega_0^2(x-d)^2-\frac{q^2E^2}{2m\omega_0^2}.
$$
Its <ground state> is $|0_E\rangle=|0\rangle+\eta|1\rangle+O(\eta^2)$ in the original basis. Adiabatic following therefore predicts exactly the finite original-basis limit found above.

The <exponential switch-on of a driven quantum oscillator> also permits an exact check. A linear force preserves a <coherent state>, with original-basis displacement $\alpha(0)=i\eta u/(1+iu)$. Thus the exact original-basis <probability> is $|\alpha|^2e^{-|\alpha|^2}$, whose weak-field limit is the expression above. In the instantaneous displaced basis the mean excitation is instead $|\alpha-\eta|^2=\eta^2/(1+u^2)$ and does tend to zero for slow switching. The two bases measure different notions of excitation.

= B3
{c}
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B3}

Take a normalized <spin one-half> state in the $S_z$ <eigenbasis>. Since $\mathbf S=(\hbar/2)\boldsymbol\sigma$, direct multiplication of the <Pauli matrices> gives
$$
\langle S_x\rangle=\frac\hbar2(\alpha^*\beta+\beta^*\alpha),\quad \langle S_y\rangle=\frac\hbar2(-i\alpha^*\beta+i\beta^*\alpha),\quad \langle S_z\rangle=\frac\hbar2(|\alpha|^2-|\beta|^2).
$$
Substitution of the half-angle parametrization gives
$$
\boxed{\langle\mathbf S\rangle=\frac\hbar2(\sin\theta\cos\phi,\sin\theta\sin\phi,\cos\theta).}
$$
Thus $\theta$ and $\phi$ are precisely the polar and azimuthal angles of the mean <spin>. Two complex coefficients contain four real parameters; normalization removes one and an unobservable common <quantum phase> removes another. The resulting pure states form the <Bloch sphere>. One may choose $\alpha$ real and nonnegative away from the patch where $\alpha=0$. At the poles the azimuth is redundant. The length $|\langle\mathbf S\rangle|=\hbar/2$ should not be confused with $\sqrt{\langle S^2\rangle}=\sqrt3\hbar/2$.

For a spatial <plane wave>, $-i\hbar\boldsymbol\nabla$ becomes $\hbar\mathbf k$, so the <Hamiltonian operator> reduces to the <Weyl Hamiltonian> <matrix>
$$
H(\mathbf k)=\hbar v\,\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf k.
$$
Define $\mathbf n=(\sin\theta\cos\phi,\sin\theta\sin\phi,\cos\theta)$ and $\chi_+=(\cos(\theta/2),e^{i\phi}\sin(\theta/2))^{\mathsf T}$. The <matrix>
$$
\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf n=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta&e^{-i\phi}\sin\theta\\e^{i\phi}\sin\theta&-\cos\theta\end{pmatrix}
$$
satisfies $(\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf n)\chi_+=\chi_+$ by the angle-addition identities. Consequently the displayed <spinor> is an <energy eigenstate> for either $\mathbf k=+k\mathbf n$ or $\mathbf k=-k\mathbf n$, with respective energies $+\hbar vk$ and $-\hbar vk$, for $k\geq0$. Equivalently, at each fixed nonzero <wavevector> the two branches are
$$
\boxed{E_\pm(\mathbf k)=\pm\hbar v|\mathbf k|\quad(v>0).}
$$
If $v<0$, the two <energy> labels exchange; the ordered <eigenvalues> are $\pm\hbar|v||\mathbf k|$. Taking $v>0$ sets the convention in the sketches. An <orthogonal> <spinor> $\chi_-=(-e^{-i\phi}\sin(\theta/2),\cos(\theta/2))^{\mathsf T}$ obeys $(\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf n)\chi_-=-\chi_-$ and supplies the other branch at $+k\mathbf n$.

The <Pauli matrix multiplication law> independently gives $H(\mathbf k)^2=(\hbar v)^2|\mathbf k|^2I$. The <dispersion relation> is therefore a pair of cones touching at zero <energy> and <momentum>; a signed straight-line cut has upper and lower branches $\pm\hbar v|k_x|$. In the $k_z=0$ plane, $\theta=\pi/2$, and the upper-branch mean <spin> points radially outwards, the lower-branch <spin> radially inwards. This is <spin-momentum locking>, not a tangential <spin> texture. At the touching point the two states are degenerate and the <spin> direction is not selected by the <Hamiltonian operator>.

\Image[2018-weyl-dispersion.png]
{title=Radial spin locking and a shifted Weyl cone, including a plane that misses its node}

Now let a spatially constant perturbation have <matrix> $V=\begin{pmatrix}u&w\\w^*&z\end{pmatrix}$, with $u,z$ real. The <Hermitian two-by-two matrix in the Pauli basis> decomposition follows explicitly from
$$
\begin{pmatrix}u&w\\w^*&z\end{pmatrix}=dI+a\sigma_x+b\sigma_y+c\sigma_z=\begin{pmatrix}d+c&a-ib\\a+ib&d-c\end{pmatrix},
$$
where $d=(u+z)/2$, $c=(u-z)/2$, $a=\operatorname{Re}w$, and $b=-\operatorname{Im}w$ are real. Equivalently, $d=\operatorname{tr}V/2$ and $(a,b,c)_i=\operatorname{tr}(V\sigma_i)/2$. The four <matrices> are <linearly independent> under the <trace> <inner product> and span the four-dimensional <real vector space> of Hermitian two-by-two <matrices>.

Spatial constancy means the perturbation does not mix different <wavevectors>, so each <momentum> sector can still be diagonalized independently. Write $\mathbf b=(a,b,c)$. The full <matrix> and its exact <eigenvalues> are
$$
H'(\mathbf k)=dI+(\hbar v\mathbf k+\mathbf b)\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma,\qquad \boxed{E_\pm(\mathbf k)=d\pm\sqrt{(\hbar vk_x+a)^2+(\hbar vk_y+b)^2+(\hbar vk_z+c)^2}.}
$$
This <constant matrix perturbation of a Weyl Hamiltonian> translates the cone to
$$
\boxed{\mathbf k_*=-\frac{(a,b,c)}{\hbar v},\qquad E_*=d.}
$$
In three-dimensional <momentum space> the <energy bands> still touch; the perturbation produces no full <spectral gap>. The new branch <spin> directions are parallel and antiparallel to $\hbar v\mathbf k+\mathbf b$, so their radial texture is centered on the shifted point.

A restricted cut must be distinguished from the full <dispersion relation>. In the fixed plane $k_z=0$, the energies are $d\pm\sqrt{(\hbar vk_x+a)^2+(\hbar vk_y+b)^2+c^2}$, with minimum branch separation $2|c|$. This plane misses the crossing if $c\ne0$. Along any line through $\mathbf k_*$ the <dispersion relation> still crosses linearly. Thus a sketch of a gapped fixed-plane cut alone would not describe the gapless three-dimensional <Hamiltonian operator>. The cone after a general perturbation is also no longer a function of $|\mathbf k|$ about the old origin alone.

= B4
{c}
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B4}

For physical <spin ladder operators>, $S_j^\pm=S_j^x\pm iS_j^y$. Operators at distinct sites commute. Expanding the two products cancels their cross terms and gives
$$
\frac12(S_j^+S_{j+1}^-+S_j^-S_{j+1}^+)=S_j^xS_{j+1}^x+S_j^yS_{j+1}^y.
$$
Adding the remaining component proves
$$
\boxed{\mathbf S_j\cdot\mathbf S_{j+1}=\frac12(S_j^+S_{j+1}^-+S_j^-S_{j+1}^+)+S_j^zS_{j+1}^z.}
$$
For <spin one-half>, $S^+|\downarrow\rangle=\hbar|\uparrow\rangle$, $S^-|\uparrow\rangle=\hbar|\downarrow\rangle$, and raising an up <spin> or lowering a down <spin> gives zero. These formulas restore the physical $\hbar$ factors implicit in the dimensionless ladder hint.

Let $|n\rangle$ abbreviate the state with the only down <spin> at site $n$, keeping $|\psi_0\rangle$ for the fully up state. If $n$ lies on neither end of bond $(j,j+1)$, that bond contains two up <spins>. The ladder terms vanish, while $S_j^zS_{j+1}^z$ gives $\hbar^2/4$. Thus
$$
\mathbf S_j\cdot\mathbf S_{j+1}|n\rangle=\frac{\hbar^2}{4}|n\rangle\qquad(n\ne j,j+1).
$$
For a down <spin> at $j$, the $z$ product is instead $-\hbar^2/4$, and $S_j^+S_{j+1}^-$ moves it to $j+1$ with <amplitude> $\hbar^2$. For a down <spin> at $j+1$, the other ladder product moves it back. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf S_j\cdot\mathbf S_{j+1}|j\rangle=\frac{\hbar^2}{4}(2|j+1\rangle-|j\rangle),\qquad \mathbf S_j\cdot\mathbf S_{j+1}|j+1\rangle=\frac{\hbar^2}{4}(2|j\rangle-|j+1\rangle).}
$$
Every bond in $|\psi_0\rangle$ is aligned, so summing its $N$ bond energies gives
$$
\boxed{H|\psi_0\rangle=E_0|\psi_0\rangle,\qquad E_0=-\frac{NJ}{4}.}
$$
This is indeed a <ground-state energy>: a pair of spin-one-half particles has $\mathbf S_j\cdot\mathbf S_{j+1}$ <eigenvalues> $\hbar^2/4$ and $-3\hbar^2/4$, so each ferromagnetic bond is bounded below by $-J/4$, simultaneously attained by the fully aligned state. The isotropic <Heisenberg model> has a whole maximum-total-spin ground multiplet, not a unique all-up <ground state>.

In the <single-excitation subspace>, only the two bonds adjacent to the down <spin> differ from the aligned background. Their diagonal <energy> increase is $J$, and each neighboring hop has <amplitude> $-J/2$. Hence, with periodic site indices,
$$
\boxed{H|n\rangle=(E_0+J)|n\rangle-\frac J2(|n-1\rangle+|n+1\rangle).}
$$
This is an exact finite-dimensional hopping problem. Choose the active one-site translation convention $T|n\rangle=|n+1\rangle$. A periodic plane-wave coefficient pattern requires $e^{iq(n+N)a}=e^{iqna}$, so
$$
\boxed{q=\frac{2\pi\ell}{Na},\qquad \ell\in\mathbb Z,\qquad q\ \text{identified modulo }\frac{2\pi}{a}.}
$$
There are $N$ independent such <wavevectors>, for example one set of $N$ consecutive integers $\ell$ in a <Brillouin zone>. The normalized <one-magnon Bloch states on a ring> are $|q\rangle=N^{-1/2}\sum_{n=1}^Ne^{iqna}|n\rangle$. Reindexing with the periodic coefficient condition gives $T|q\rangle=e^{-iqa}|q\rangle$ and hence $T^N|q\rangle=|q\rangle$.

There is a logical qualification to the condition stated in the paper. On an $N$-site ring $T^N=I$ already holds for every state. In particular, the finite sum written with an arbitrary real $q$ also returns to itself after $N$ translations; that condition alone does not quantize $q$. The displayed allowed values are the ones for which the ansatz is a single one-site translation <eigenstate>, equivalently a consistently periodic <plane wave>. This is the additional condition needed for the subsequent energy-eigenstate argument. Other $q$ values generally describe superpositions of the allowed <momentum> modes.

Apply the hopping <Hamiltonian operator> and shift the summation index using <periodic boundary condition>. The coefficients from $|n-1\rangle$ and $|n+1\rangle$ are respectively $e^{iqa}$ and $e^{-iqa}$ times the original coefficient. Thus
$$
H|q\rangle=\left(E_0+J-\frac J2e^{iqa}-\frac J2e^{-iqa}\right)|q\rangle,
$$
and the <nearest-neighbour ferromagnetic magnon dispersion> is
$$
\boxed{E(q)=E_0+J[1-\cos(qa)]=E_0+2J\sin^2\frac{qa}{2}.}
$$
The unnormalized state in the question has the same <energy>. Distinct allowed <momenta> are <orthogonal>, since $N^{-1}\sum_ne^{i(q-q')na}=\delta_{qq'}$ over one set of representatives.

\Image[2018-magnon-dispersion.png]
{title=Exact one-magnon dispersion and its discrete ring momenta over two reciprocal periods}

For large $N$ the sampled <wavevectors> become dense, and the requested sketch is the periodic curve $[E(q)-E_0]/J=1-\cos(qa)$ over $-2\pi<qa<2\pi$. It has a zero at $q=0$ and tends to the equivalent zero at either open endpoint; maxima $2J$ occur at $q=\pm\pi/a$. Near any equivalent zero,
$$
\boxed{E(q)-E_0=\frac{Ja^2q^2}{2}+O(q^4),}
$$
using $q$ measured from that zero. The low-momentum <ferromagnetic magnon> is quadratic, with <group velocity> $\hbar^{-1}dE/dq=(Ja/\hbar)\sin(qa)$. The $q=0$ state is proportional to the total <spin lowering operator> acting on $|\psi_0\rangle$, so it belongs to the same ground multiplet and has exactly zero excitation <energy>. Extending the plot beyond one reciprocal period repeats states rather than adding new independent modes. The lattice spacing $a$ is the nearest-neighbor chord length specified in the paper; $Na$ is the translation length in this lattice description, not necessarily the continuous circumference of a circular arrangement.
